    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d381">

                <pb facs="0569" n="479" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg479"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1111 Stam, John C./8: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé in
                        China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-12-13T13:00:00-05:00">December 13, 1934—1
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>384. Nanking’s 84, December 13, 7 p.m.,<anchor n="80" xml:id="d381-f1"/>
                    reporting that the bodies of Mr. and Mrs. Stam have been found but that the baby
                    has not been found.</p>
                <pb facs="0570" n="480" xml:id="pg480"/>
                <p>Department assumes that Legation will make immediate representations to the
                    Chinese Government expressing this Government’s deep concern, and requesting
                    that prompt and vigorous steps be taken to effect the rescue of the child, if
                    alive, and to apprehend and punish the culprits in accordance with law.</p>
                <p>Report by telegraph.</p>
                <p>Department has informed Stam’s father of Nanking’s telegram.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
